{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head f583fcac6f34108ef0685b083405a3092b0545db, citing both artifacts: f8a8a660 at internal/workroom and 00c6eefb at internal/app.\n\nOne blocker, and it is the condition rather than the code. The normalization itself is correct — I checked every legacy interpretation by hand and then tried to break it — but the request asks that app preflight no longer own a duplicate vocabulary, and it still owns one.\n\nThe blocker. internal/app/app.go:496, in AddActor, still reads `kind != \"human\" \u0026\u0026 kind != \"agent\" \u0026\u0026 kind != \"service\"`. That is the same three words the seam now owns, asked as the same question: is this one of the descriptive kinds. validateAuthorityRole was the copy that got removed; this one was left. Add a fourth kind and both places have to change, and only one of them has a test pinning the vocabulary — which is precisely the drift a single seam exists to prevent.\n\nI am not going to dictate the remedy, because the obvious one has a wrinkle worth your judgement. RosterRoleIsKind would answer this correctly today, but its name and doc comment say it classifies a legacy role word, and AddActor is validating a kind field, so reusing it reads oddly at the call site. A second small accessor over the same normalizer, named for kinds, would say what it means. Either is fine, and so is telling me these are genuinely two vocabularies that happen to share three words — but then say so in the comment, because the next reader will see a duplicate.\n\nEverything else holds, and I checked the compatibility claim rather than accepting it.\n\nThe seam is behaviour-preserving. I worked the truth table by hand for both old functions before running anything. rosterMembership was `kind == \"\" || role == \"participant\"` and normalized.membership is the same expression. rosterAuthorityRole matches case for case: legacy agent/human/service to empty, legacy operator to operator, legacy participant to empty via the trailing rule after the default branch sets kind to unspecified, modern participant to empty, everything else through unchanged. The empty-role case returns empty on both sides. RosterRoleIsKind is true for exactly agent, human and service, which makes the new validateAuthorityRole reject exactly the old set — empty, participant, agent, human, service — so no authority word gains or loses standing.\n\naddRoleGrant is the part most likely to hide a difference, because the old code chose membership in a three-branch cascade and the new code computes it as one expression, and I checked each branch. Legacy records still come out membership true with kind derived and role participant, except operator which keeps its authority and gains kind human. Modern records are membership when role is participant or the grant is unratified, which is the genesis seed case, and membershipBasis is still set only when the grant is neither, and only when there is a basis to set. The new guard `!grant.membership \u0026\u0026 len(restsOn) != 0` is the same predicate the old third branch reached.\n\nThen I stopped reasoning and tried to break it, because a table that reads right can still be pinned by tests that look elsewhere. I mutated the seam four ways in a detached worktree of my own — never your branch — and reran the suites. Making legacy operator normalize to unspecified instead of human: caught. Dropping `role == \"participant\"` from the membership expression: caught. Removing the rule that clears authority for participant: caught. Widening RosterRoleIsKind to admit operator: caught. All four fail the tests, so the mutation-pinning claim is real rather than asserted.\n\nThat fourth mutation is the one I care about, and it is worth naming which way the danger runs. Because validateAuthorityRole rejects whatever RosterRoleIsKind accepts, a wrongly-wide classifier refuses a legitimate operator grant, which fails closed; a wrongly-narrow one lets a kind word be granted as authority, which is inert because nothing tests hasRole for agent, human or service. So neither direction is an escalation, and the byte-pinned projections and precondition pins both pass unchanged, which is the evidence that no historical record reads differently under the new seam.\n\nArchitecture: internal/workroom and internal/app. The layer contract is preserved and, in one respect, tightened — the fold now owns the roster vocabulary outright and the application consumes it through an exported predicate, which is the direction docs/reference/architecture.md already describes, with interpretation belonging to the workroom layer. No boundary moves and that page does not need to change.\n\nSecurity: this is authority code, so I treated the compatibility claim as the security claim. Beneficiary self-grant and self-ratification are still refused, operator standing is still required to ratify an operator grant and to change operator-bearing membership, and both checks now read the normalized role rather than a locally recomputed one — which removes the possibility of the ratify path and the supersede path disagreeing about what a legacy record means. The dormant-grant handling in membershipCarriesOperator is untouched. I found no way to make a legacy record gain or lose operator standing across the change.\n\nSimplification: the change is a simplification and the right one — one seam replacing two hand-rolled readings plus an application copy. The blocker above is the remaining half of it. Beyond that I found nothing worth cutting; the normalizedRosterState struct earns its keep by returning three related facts that every caller needs together, and computing them separately is what allowed them to drift in the first place.\n\nOne practical note for merge rather than for the verdict. This head is one ahead of main and two behind it, because the unreachable-UI deletion landed while the review was open. Those commits touch ui and internal/service only, so there is no overlap; I confirmed it merges clean into main with merge-tree. It will still need carrying before merge, and gs merge will want a verdict over the carried head.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet silent, go build ./... clean, internal/workroom and internal/app pass, both pass under -race, the full suite passes, git diff --check clean against main, worktree clean.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f8a8a6607aedfc5573732611f7f13a5c22744290","head":"f583fcac6f34108ef0685b083405a3092b0545db","verdict":"changes-requested"}}